speechtext:
Mr. Speaker, it gives me pleasure to present a petition pursuant to Standing Order 106, which petition was signed by constituents from my riding and visitors to the Byward market in lower town Ottawa, also in my constituency, who have had an opportunity to discuss with us this summer the Government proposal to impose a tax on food.
The petition reads as follows:
"Whereas since September, 1984, the federal Government has imposed on Canadian taxpayers 25 new taxes or tax increases, in particular on medical and dental supplies, snack foods, candy and soft drinks;
Whereas the federal Government in its tax reform proposal has studied the possibility of imposing on Canadians a general tax on goods and services, including food;
And whereas food constitutes a vjtal element of day-to-day life and imposition of this tax would have the effect of seriously penalizing certain categories of taxpayers, such as the aged, families, the unemployed, low income taxpayers, youths and students;
Wherefore your petitioners humbly pray and call upon Parliament to reject all measures that would have the effect of imposing a sales tax on food products.
